## Galaxy Interaction

On mouse movement:

- Near particles respond more strongly than distant particles.
- The camera shifts slightly using damped interpolation.
- The galaxy exhibits restrained perspective parallax.
- Nearby stars gently drift away from or orbit around the cursor.
- The central galaxy remains visually stable.

On mouse click:

- Generate a short-lived radial energy disturbance.
- Nearby particles gently expand and return to their orbital behavior.
- Add a faint violet/gold pulse.
- Avoid explosions, flashes, or excessive visual noise.

On scrolling:

- Camera depth and galaxy rotation respond gradually.
- The apparent distance between foreground and background changes.
- Galaxy lighting evolves with page progression.
- The camera must not rotate excessively or induce motion sickness.

Create a visually convincing glasses-free depth illusion using perspective, motion parallax, occlusion, and lighting.

Do not claim to implement hardware stereoscopic 3D.

## Performance

Use one reusable galaxy scene wherever practical.

Avoid creating separate expensive WebGL scenes for every section.

Use instancing, point buffers, and GPU-efficient effects where appropriate.

Clamp device pixel ratio based on device capability.

Pause unnecessary rendering work when the page is hidden.

Release GPU resources and event listeners properly.

Handle WebGL initialization failure and context loss.

Provide a graceful static or lightweight animated fallback if WebGL is unavailable.

